{"lc": 1, "type": "constructor", "id": ["langchain", "schema", "document", "Document"], "kwargs": {"metadata": {"source": "data\\documents\\pdf\\Fiona Stanley Hospital Dermatology-acne-guidelines-2025.pdf", "filename": "Fiona Stanley Hospital Dermatology-acne-guidelines-2025.pdf", "filepath": "data\\documents\\pdf\\Fiona Stanley Hospital Dermatology-acne-guidelines-2025.pdf", "page": 2, "title": "Fiona Stanley Hospital Dermatology pre-referral guidelines \u2013 acne", "year": "2025", "producer": "Adobe PDF Library 21.5.90", "creator": "Acrobat PDFMaker 21 for Word", "creationdate": "2025-05-20T10:37:28+08:00", "author": "Fiona Stanley Hospital Dermatology", "company": "WA Health", "contenttypeid": "0x010100D20558782E22444F8E6476743C0A7A47", "keywords": "Fiona Stanley Hospital, FSH, dermatology, pre-referral guidelines, acne", "moddate": "2025-05-20T10:37:29+08:00", "sourcemodified": "D:20250520023710", "subject": "Inofrmation on referring, treatment, introduction to acne", "_dlc_dociditemguid": "400bd398-572f-4117-a0d8-763933fbd180", "total_pages": 6, "page_label": "3"}, "page_content": "Dermatology pre-referral guidelines - acne  May 2025 Page 3 of 6 \nSpecific measures \nAcne treatments often take at least 6-12 weeks before improvement is noted regardless of the \ntreatment method. Therefore, as long as therapies are tolerated, it is important to trial these for at \nleast 3-months. \nMild acne: Non-prescription options \nFor mild acne, the following over-the-counter treatments can be recommended as first-line \ntherapy. \n\u2022 Azelaic acid 20% lotion (AzClear Lotion\u00ae) \no This should be used as a field therapy to the acne-prone areas once or twice daily.  \no It can be helpful for early comedonal acne, as well as mild papulopustular acne. \no This treatment also has some fading qualities, making it particularly useful for \npatients prone to post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation. \no It is TGA pregnancy category B1, and compatible with breast-feeding. \n\u2022 Benzoyl peroxide comes in many different preparations ranging from 2.5-10% \no Preparations include gel, cream and body wash. \no Choose a preparation that suits the patient depending on their history and location of \nacne. \no The gel and cream can be used as a spot therapy for inflamed acne lesions applied \ndaily until settled.", "type": "Document"}}